### RAR + Consent Portal — IS 7.3 HTTP Exchange
### Lab: Day 18 — RAR + Consent Portal
###
### All credential values are PLACEHOLDERS — replace before use.
### This file traces the full consent lifecycle: PAR → consent approval → token → revocation.

###############################################################################
### PART 1: PAR REQUEST WITH authorization_details
###############################################################################

### Step 1: Push Authorization Request (PAR) with authorization_details
# The TPP sends the authorization request directly to IS 7.3 via PAR (RFC 9126).
# This prevents leaking authorization_details via the browser URL.
#
# QUESTION: Why is authorization_details sent in a PAR body request rather than in the browser URL?
# QUESTION: What does IS 7.3 return, and how does the TPP use it?
# NOTE: client_assertion is form body params, NOT Authorization: Bearer header

POST https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/oauth2/par
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

client_id=<PLACEHOLDER: tpp-client-id>
&client_assertion_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer
&client_assertion=<PLACEHOLDER: private_key_jwt signed with TPP private key>
&response_type=code
&scope=openid+payments
&redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Ftpp.example.com%2Fcallback
&code_challenge=<PLACEHOLDER: base64url(sha256(code_verifier))>
&code_challenge_method=S256
&authorization_details=%5B%7B%22type%22%3A%22payment_initiation%22%2C%22instructedAmount%22%3A%7B%22currency%22%3A%22EUR%22%2C%22amount%22%3A%22150.00%22%7D%2C%22creditorName%22%3A%22<PLACEHOLDER%3A+Merchant+Name>%22%2C%22creditorAccount%22%3A%7B%22iban%22%3A%22<PLACEHOLDER%3A+IBAN>%22%7D%7D%5D

###
### Decoded authorization_details (for readability):
### [
###   {
###     "type": "payment_initiation",
###     "instructedAmount": { "currency": "EUR", "amount": "150.00" },
###     "creditorName": "<PLACEHOLDER: Merchant Name>",
###     "creditorAccount": { "iban": "<PLACEHOLDER: creditor-IBAN>" },
###     "debtorAccount": { "iban": "<PLACEHOLDER: debtor-IBAN>" },
###     "remittanceInformationUnstructured": "<PLACEHOLDER: payment reference>"
###   }
### ]
###
### Expected response — HTTP 201 Created
### {
###   "request_uri": "urn:ietf:params:oauth:request_uri:<PLACEHOLDER: unique-id>",
###   "expires_in": 90
### }
###
### NOTE: The TPP must use this request_uri in the authorization redirect within 90 seconds.
### NOTE: The full authorization_details never appears in the browser URL — only the opaque request_uri.

###############################################################################

### Step 2: Authorization redirect (reference — browser navigation, not an API call)
# The TPP redirects the user's browser to IS 7.3 using the request_uri.
# IS 7.3 retrieves the stored PAR object, including authorization_details.
# IS 7.3 then redirects to the consent portal, passing authorization_details.
#
# Browser URL (conceptual — not an HTTP client call):
# GET https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/oauth2/authorize
#     ?request_uri=urn:ietf:params:oauth:request_uri:<PLACEHOLDER>
#     &client_id=<PLACEHOLDER: tpp-client-id>
#
# IS 7.3 then redirects internally to:
# GET /authenticationendpoint/oauth2_consent.do
#     ?client=<PLACEHOLDER: tpp-client-id>
#     &authorization_details=<URL-encoded JSON>
#     &scope=openid+payments
#     &sessionDataKey=<PLACEHOLDER: session-key>
#
# QUESTION: How does the consent portal template access `creditorName` from authorization_details?
# QUESTION: What happens in IS 7.3 after the user clicks "Approve" in the consent portal?

###############################################################################
### PART 2: CONSENT MANAGEMENT API
###############################################################################

### Step 3: List active consents for a user
# After the user approves, IS 7.3 stores a consent record. This call retrieves it.
# Useful for: consent receipts, user-facing consent management UI, compliance audits.
#
# QUESTION: What fields does IS 7.3 return in the consent record?
# QUESTION: Which field links this consent to the authorization_details type?

GET https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/api/identity/consent-mgt/v1.0/consents?userId=<PLACEHOLDER: user@banking.example.com>&clientId=<PLACEHOLDER: tpp-client-id>&state=ACTIVE
Authorization: Bearer <PLACEHOLDER: admin-token>
Accept: application/json

###
### Expected response — HTTP 200 OK
### [
###   {
###     "consentId": "<PLACEHOLDER: uuid>",
###     "userId": "<PLACEHOLDER: user@banking.example.com>",
###     "clientId": "<PLACEHOLDER: tpp-client-id>",
###     "consentType": "payment_initiation",
###     "state": "ACTIVE",
###     "createdTime": "<PLACEHOLDER: ISO-8601>",
###     "updatedTime": "<PLACEHOLDER: ISO-8601>",
###     "consentAttributes": {
###       "instructedAmount_currency": "EUR",
###       "instructedAmount_amount": "150.00",
###       "creditorName": "<PLACEHOLDER: Merchant Name>",
###       "creditorAccount_iban": "<PLACEHOLDER: creditor-IBAN>",
###       "debtorAccount_iban": "<PLACEHOLDER: debtor-IBAN>"
###     }
###   }
### ]
###
### NOTE: This is the machine-readable consent receipt. PSD2 audits require this to be producible
###       on demand for any active or historical consent.

###############################################################################

### Step 4: Revoke a specific consent
# The user revokes only the payment initiation consent.
# Account information consent (if any) remains active — selective revocation.
#
# QUESTION: What state does the consent record move to after this DELETE?
# QUESTION: Is the existing access token immediately invalid after this call?

DELETE https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/api/identity/consent-mgt/v1.0/consents/<PLACEHOLDER: consentId>
Authorization: Bearer <PLACEHOLDER: admin-token>

###
### Expected response — HTTP 200 OK (no body, or confirmation message)
###
### The consent record state is now REVOKED.
### IS 7.3 does NOT immediately revoke issued access tokens — they remain valid until `exp`.
### Only the refresh token grant is blocked (checked at grant time, not at resource server).

###############################################################################

### Step 5: Attempt token refresh after consent revocation
# The TPP's access token has expired. It tries to get a new one with the refresh token.
# IS 7.3 checks the consent record state before issuing a new access token.
#
# QUESTION: What does IS 7.3 check before issuing a new access token from a refresh token?
# QUESTION: What HTTP status and error code does IS 7.3 return?
# NOTE: client_assertion is form body params, NOT Authorization: Bearer header

POST https://<PLACEHOLDER: is-host>/oauth2/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=refresh_token
&refresh_token=<PLACEHOLDER: refresh-token-from-authorization-code-grant>
&client_id=<PLACEHOLDER: tpp-client-id>
&client_assertion_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer
&client_assertion=<PLACEHOLDER: fresh private_key_jwt>

###
### Expected response — HTTP 400 Bad Request
### {
###   "error": "consent_revoked",
###   "error_description": "Consent has been revoked"
### }
###
### IS 7.3 checks: find consent record linked to this refresh token → state is REVOKED →
### reject the token grant with consent_revoked.
###
### The TPP must surface this error to the user: "Your payment consent has been revoked."
### The TPP cannot re-authorize silently — a new user interaction is required to create new consent.
